From a13b24a529129049d88147a24eba9b9fbef53025 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:43:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] network/TCPServer: keep a connection open after a
 notification

A JSON-RPC notification, a request without an "id", or a batch made only
of them, has no answer: CJSONRPC::MethodCall() returns an empty string.
Since the server queues its output, that empty string went into the queue
and to send() with no bytes, whose 0 is taken for a failed write, so the
connection was dropped with "Send failed, dropping 0 bytes", and the
client lost the requests and announcements that would have followed on it.

An empty message is now nothing to queue. WebSocket clients are not
affected: their empty answer is a frame of two bytes, sent as before.

Reported upstream as a regression of the per-connection workers
(xbmc/xbmc#29886).
---
 xbmc/network/TCPServer.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/network/TCPServer.cpp b/xbmc/network/TCPServer.cpp
index 95298f3..0d84f0a 100644
--- a/xbmc/network/TCPServer.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/network/TCPServer.cpp
@@ -675,7 +675,10 @@ bool CTCPServer::CTCPClient::SetAnnouncementFlags(int flags)
 
 void CTCPServer::CTCPClient::Send(const char *data, unsigned int size)
 {
-  if (m_disconnecting)
+  // A notification, or a batch of them, has no answer: MethodCall() returns an empty one, and
+  // there is nothing to write. Queued, it would go to send() with no bytes, whose 0 is taken for
+  // a failed write and drops a connection the client still uses.
+  if (size == 0 || m_disconnecting)
     return;
 
   {
